# Important: Companies House does NOT solve sole traders

This is the big caveat.

Companies House is principally the incorporated-company world. It will miss huge chunks of the people you actually care about: self-employed electricians, plumbers, repair technicians, builders, etc.

There is **no complete public database of every UK sole trader**. DBT explicitly says there is no single UK database covering every active business. Its Business Population Estimates combine VAT/PAYE-register information with Labour Force Survey estimates for businesses below those registration thresholds. ([GOV.UK][2])

And this matters enormously: at the start of 2025 the official estimate was about **3.2 million sole proprietorships**, versus about 2.1 million actively trading companies. ([GOV.UK][3])

So for POW:

```text
Companies House
= incorporated physical capacity

DBT/ONS + labour datasets
= total / sole-trader capacity
```

Use DBT's **Business Population Estimates** as the calibration layer. It explicitly breaks businesses down by legal status, industry, geography and employment. ([GOV.UK][4])

ONS also publishes quarterly low-level geographic business creation/closure data. ([Office for National Statistics][5])

And apprenticeships give you the future supply pipeline; the DfE release lets you download underlying datasets by subject/provider/geography. ([Explore Education Statistics][6])

So one killer POW series becomes:

```text
ELECTRICIAN CAPACITY

incorporated electrician businesses
+
estimated sole-trader population
+
apprentices entering
+
business formations
+
business failures
+
capital raised
```

Now we're actually measuring whether Britain can produce enough electricians for an electrification / data-centre / robotics buildout.

